- Optimize input handling performance (no chug/lag),
- Make the 'compass' a compass,
- Chat panel?
- Right-click context menu with "Copy" or "Dictionary" for individual words?
- ResetClock: re-derive the FES-suppression model from the wire. The engine assumes a ~500ms
  server rate limit; the corpus contradicts a time-based cause (547 stats-container pairs land
  <500ms apart, 58 under 250ms, two probes sent in the same millisecond were both answered).
  Clio's FES.md says the score packet is sent when a stat CHANGES. LogResetDiagnostics defaults
  to false, so the theory cannot be falsified in normal use.
- Combat: the 07 "isolated hit" family (bites/stings/kicks/throws) is unparsed, so a wyvern's
  venomous tail sting - real damage, its own code, its own "Stamina=N/M." line - is invisible to
  per-fight stats.
- Combat rail: CombatTierResolver.CriticalStaminaFloorTier (the "no lower than T2 at or below
  6.5 stamina" floor in docs/combat-panel-design.md) exists and is tested but nothing calls it;
  SidePanelViewModel feeds the stamina tier straight to ResolvePulseTier.
- $eval (Core/ItemEvalSession.cs): its Handler mutates state/description/weighLine/results/names
  from the connection's raise thread while the awaiting code reads them from the UI thread, with
  no synchronization. Now reachable on Android, where ARM's weaker memory model makes the
  unsynchronized read a worse bet than it was on x86. The poll loop (:264) also has no
  ConfigureAwait(false), so ~27 continuations bounce onto the UI thread per identify.
- $eval on Android writes items.jsonl into the cache directory (ClogPaths.GetClogDirectory),
  which the OS evicts under pressure, so measurements do not accumulate there. Stage 5's
  one-database design should give it a durable home.
- Any "$" or "^" line is client-local on both platforms, including at MUD2's persona password
  prompt, where it is echoed into scrollback and history and never sent. There is no escape
  hatch for sending one to the MUD.
- Mapping: MappingStore.IsTransientRefusal matches only "blocked by". Movement refusals come in
  three families - hard no-exit ("You cannot go north from here."), blocked by an entity
  (transient), and terrain/conditional ("The forest becomes too dense...", "Rain has swollen the
  river...", "The gap is too narrow... without dropping everything") which are weather- or
  encumbrance-gated and can succeed on retry. The third family is recorded as no-edge.
